          "distinct": true,
          "id": "ce9bc684556a46cf30f7088e5b7badac55aaedb7",
          "message": "fix(engine): a rejected request never reached the client waiting on it (#2041)\n\nA sequence the scheduler refuses before it runs is marked FINISHED and\nrouted through `_rejected`, which leaves the engine as an ADD message.\n`CoreManager`'s ADD branch serves the offline `llm.generate()` queue and\nnever touches `_seq_id_to_callback`, so an online client's callback was\nnever invoked: the SSE stream stayed open with no chunk ever sent and the\nrequest hung until the client gave up. The path was built for aborts,\nwhere the client is already gone, and for offline generation, which reads\nthe queue -- an unschedulable prompt is the one case where an online\nclient is still waiting.\n\nDeliver the terminal output there when a callback is registered, and flush\nthe batch it buffers into, without which the chunk never leaves the\nthread-local. Callback registration is the exact test for \"someone is\nwaiting\": `add_request` registers online requests and offline ones have\nnone, and a normal completion has had its callback popped by the STREAM\nthat delivered it, so both keep their existing path.\n\nReport it as an error rather than an empty completion where that is still\npossible. The pool ceiling is measured in the engine subprocess, so the\nAPI server's Config never learns it; publish it on READY, keep the\nsmallest across DP ranks, and check it beside `max_model_len` so an\noversized prompt is refused with a 400 while the client is still being\nanswered instead of after the scheduler discovers it.\n\n`BlockManager.max_pool_tokens` bisects `num_pool_blocks` instead of\ninverting it: under block-level interleaving a rank's share is not a\nplain `seq_len / dcp_world_size`, and a hand-derived inverse would drift\nfrom the allocator.\n\nSigned-off-by: whx-sjtu <xiaowang990929@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-27T20:42:34+08:00",
